<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1729–<a href="/years/1782">1782</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Kalaniopuu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kalaniʻōpuʻu (c. 1729–1782) was ruler (aliʻi nui) of the island of Hawaiʻi from the 1750s until his death. He fought long wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> and its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahekili-ii">Kahekili II</a>. In 1779 he received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kealakekua-bay">Kealakekua Bay</a>, and Cook was killed after trying to take him hostage. He named his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiwalao">Kiwalaʻō</a> as heir and his nephew Kamehameha as guardian of the war god Kūkaʻilimoku, setting the stage for civil war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu.jpg" alt="Kalaniʻōpuʻu" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark Miller · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalani%CA%BB%C5%8Dpu%CA%BBu_%CA%BBAhu_%CA%BBula_and_mahiole.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
